# =============================================================================
# THE DEVELOPMENT TREE, WHICH IS NOT PUBLISHED
# =============================================================================
#
# These three directories are how the library is built, proven and documented
# to ourselves. They are not part of what a user of the library consumes, and
# they are not published.
#
#   internal/   the plan, the conformance matrix, the interoperability
#               evidence and the working notes - and internal/test/, the
#               development suite (sanitizers, fuzzing, soak, the loopback
#               peer), which leans on this machine's tools
#   docker/testing/  the interoperability rig and every load test: five
#               brokers, the TLS matrix, the third-party client harnesses,
#               the Kafka and TBMQ comparisons, the board builds.
#               docker/ itself IS published: the image and the example stacks.
#   scripts/    the checks that hold the documentation and the layering to
#               account, the footprint measurement, and the Arduino shim the
#               first two compile against
#
# WHAT THIS COSTS, so it is a decision rather than a surprise:
#
#   - a clone of the published repository cannot run the fuzzer, the soak,
#     the interoperability rig, or any of the scripted checks. CMake adds
#     internal/test/ only when it is there
#   - docs/ names these checks but no longer links into them
#
# test/ IS published: a self-contained suite - the protocol, the broker, the
# server, regressions - that needs nothing but a compiler, and that GitHub
# runs on Linux, macOS and Windows.
#
# Everything a user needs - src/, examples/, docs/, test/, CMakeLists.txt,
# library.properties - is published.
internal/
docker/testing/
scripts/

# An example app's installed packages (npm install).
node_modules/
